<!--
  One member of a group, filed in the United States, with illustrative figures. Read it
  with merge-pt-member.xml; neither file says much alone.

  The pair answers a question no single document can raise: what happens when a
  consolidator puts two filings side by side. Four cases are arranged across the two
  files, and `tests/composition.rs` is where they are checked:

    * `labour` here is `pessoal` there: the same layer, named in two languages by two
      entities that each wrote their own filing.

    * `compute` here is `compute` there, and they are not the same layer: a different
      vendor, a different contract, a different unit. The names match by accident.

    * `capacidade-instalada` is only there. A member may hold a layer its sibling does
      not, and that is not an error in either filing.

    * `shift-line` here is `linha-partilhada` there, and it is one machine. Both members
      file its full ten shifts as their own nameplate, and both are right to; both show
      slack. The group is short, because it has one line and not two, and no arithmetic
      over these two documents alone can find that out.

  Nothing in this file is written to make the merge hard. Both entities filed honestly,
  in their own language, about their own business. The difficulty is in the structure,
  and that is the point.
-->
<pm:processModulus xmlns:pm="https://example.invalid/process-flow/1.0">

  <!-- Which filing this is: the URN a part or a dependence cites to point at this
       document. -->
  <pm:notation>
    <pm:uri>urn:example:filing:us-member:2026-08-31</pm:uri>
  </pm:notation>
  <pm:evidence>
